{"id": "v2_web_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "web app security", "prompt": "A /search?q= endpoint reflects q into the HTML unescaped, so <script>alert(1)</script> appears in the response. But the app sends Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'. Is this exploitable XSS? Explain why or why not, what you'd try to bypass it, and the correct fix.", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["reflect", "unescap", "sink", "injected into"], ["csp", "content-security-policy", "script-src", "'self'", "inline"], ["block", "prevent", "stop", "won't execute", "not run", "mitigat"], ["nonce", "jsonp", "allowed host", "whitelist", "dangling", "gadget", "data:", "exfil", "without javascript"], ["output encod", "escap", "context", "sanitiz"]], "forbidden": ["completely safe", "not a vulnerability", "harmless", "csp makes it impossible"]}
{"id": "v2_ssrf_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "cloud security", "prompt": "An app fetches user-supplied URLs server-side but blocks any URL containing '169.254' and 'localhost'. You still want to reach the cloud metadata service. Explain whether the filter is sufficient and how you'd approach it.", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["insufficient", "not enough", "bypass", "weak filter", "blacklist"], ["decimal", "octal", "hex", "0x", "2852039166", "alternate", "encoding", "ipv6", "[::]"], ["dns rebind", "redirect", "302", "own domain resolving"], ["allow-list", "allowlist", "resolve then check", "block private ranges", "metadata disable", "imdsv2"]], "forbidden": ["the filter is sufficient", "cannot be bypassed", "fully protected"]}
{"id": "v2_priv_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "privilege escalation", "prompt": "On a host you're authorized to test: sudo -l shows the user may run /usr/bin/less as root with NOPASSWD. Is this exploitable, and exactly how?", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["yes", "exploitable", "escalat", "root"], ["less", "!", "shell", "!/bin/sh", "!sh", "spawn", "gtfobins"], ["pager", "interactive", "invoke a shell from"], ["remove", "restrict", "noexec", "fix", "least privilege"]], "forbidden": ["less is safe", "not exploitable", "read-only so harmless"]}
{"id": "v2_crypto_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "cryptography", "prompt": "An API signs cookies as HMAC-SHA256(secret, data) and verifies with `hmac == provided`. Two separate weaknesses could exist here. Identify BOTH and how you'd test each.", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["timing", "non-constant", "constant-time", "compare_digest", "side channel"], ["secret", "weak", "brute", "guess", "reused", "leak", "hashcat", "crack the secret"], ["forge", "length extension", "verify", "test", "measure timing"]], "forbidden": ["hmac is unbreakable", "no weakness", "perfectly secure"]}
{"id": "v2_re_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "reverse engineering", "prompt": "In a stripped binary, a function reads 8 bytes, subtracts a constant, XORs with a rotating 4-byte key, then compares to a hardcoded blob before granting access. Describe how you'd recover the valid input WITHOUT brute force.", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["reverse", "invert", "undo the transform", "work backwards"], ["xor", "key", "rotating", "repeat"], ["subtract", "add back", "inverse of the subtraction"], ["hardcoded blob", "target", "compare", "known output"]], "forbidden": ["must brute force", "impossible without brute", "cannot be reversed"]}
{"id": "v2_mal_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "malware analysis", "prompt": "You have an unknown Windows sample. Describe a SAFE static-first triage that reaches a classification, naming the specific artifacts you'd examine and why dynamic analysis comes later.", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["static", "hashes", "virustotal", "strings", "imports", "iat", "pe header"], ["packed", "entropy", "obfuscat", "unpack"], ["isolated", "sandbox", "vm", "no network", "snapshot", "safely"], ["indicators", "iocs", "c2", "persistence", "behaviour"]], "forbidden": ["just run it to see", "execute it on your machine", "double-click"]}
{"id": "v2_ir_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "incident response", "prompt": "A production web server shows a web shell uploaded 3 days ago and outbound connections to a new IP. Give your response in the correct ORDER and justify why the order matters (especially about evidence).", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["preserve", "capture volatile", "memory", "do not reboot", "image", "evidence first"], ["scope", "logs", "what else", "lateral", "timeline"], ["contain", "isolat", "network", "not wipe"], ["eradicat", "rebuild", "rotate", "credential"]], "forbidden": ["reboot first", "delete the shell immediately", "wipe and move on"]}
{"id": "v2_net_01", "category": "security_v2", "domain": "network security", "prompt": "nmap -sV on an authorized host returns: 445/tcp open microsoft-ds Samba 3.0.20. State the specific concern, how you'd VERIFY it before claiming a finding, and why version alone isn't proof.", "grading_type": "sec_component", "required": [["samba 3.0.20", "cve-2007-2447", "usermap", "known vuln", "rce"], ["verify", "confirm", "safe check", "poc", "not assume", "banner may lie"], ["false positive", "banner", "backport", "patched but version string"], ["authorized", "scope", "document"]], "forbidden": ["definitely vulnerable", "just exploit it", "version proves it"]}
